          </tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q4">
    <h2>Q4 · Implant fingerprint (orphan + tmp)</h2>
    <p class="lede">Records carrying <em>both</em> <code class="sig warn">exec_target_orphan</code> and <code class="sig warn">interpreter_in_tmp</code>. The conjunction is very rare in legitimate configuration; singletons here deserve immediate triage even before correlating with the LFA queries.</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q4">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q4">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>host</th><th>module</th><th>site_class</th>
          <th>locus</th><th>exec_target</th><th>signals</th><th>snippet</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr><td colspan="7" style="text-align:center;color:var(--fg-mute)">no records match the implant fingerprint — clean fleet</td></tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q5">
    <h2>Q5 · Signal distribution by module</h2>
    <p class="lede">For each <code>(module, signal)</code> pair: how many hosts fire it, how many records carry it. Signals that fire on few hosts are inherently interesting; signals that fire on most hosts are baselines used to <em>calibrate</em> other queries.</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q5">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q5">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>module</th><th>signal</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">hosts</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">records</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr>
            <td>cron</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">exec_target_orphan</span></td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>cron</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">interpreter_in_tmp</span></td>
            <td class="num">2</td>
            <td class="num">2</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>cron</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">non_package_path</span></td>
            <td class="num">4</td>
            <td class="num">4</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>profile</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">non_package_path</span></td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>systemd</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">exec_target_orphan</span></td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
            <td class="num">1</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>systemd</td>
            <td><span class="sig warn">non_package_path</span></td>
            <td class="num">2</td>
            <td class="num">2</td>
          </tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q6">
    <h2>Q6 · Cross-host exec_target stacking (worm pattern)</h2>
    <p class="lede">Records on different hosts whose persistence text differs but whose <code>exec_target_sha256</code> matches: identical <strong>non-package</strong> payload, polymorphic persistence. Packaged binaries shared fleet-wide are excluded (they are baseline, not worms); on large fleets a <code>10%-of-hosts</code> ceiling further trims baseline, and singletons stay Q1 turf.</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q6">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q6">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>exec_target_sha256</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">hosts</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">records</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">modules</th>
          <th>sample_target</th>
          <th>spread</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr><td colspan="6" style="text-align:center;color:var(--fg-mute)">no cross-host worm pattern detected</td></tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q7">
    <h2>Q7 · Implant roll-call (cross-cutting union)</h2>
    <p class="lede">The single highest-signal query in the cookbook. One row per record that matches <em>any</em> of the canonical implant patterns: orphan+tmp, sudoers backdoors, uid=0 backdoors, kmod install replacements, profile/motd curl|wget content, sysv scripts referencing /tmp. The clauses are deliberately disjoint — every implant lands on exactly one row.</p>
    <div class="sev-summary">
      <span class="item"><span class="sev critical">critical</span> <span class="count">0</span></span>
      <span class="item"><span class="sev high">high</span> <span class="count">0</span></span>
      <span class="item"><span class="sev medium">medium</span> <span class="count">0</span></span>
      <span class="item"><span class="sev low">low</span> <span class="count">0</span></span>
      <span class="item"><span class="sev info">info</span> <span class="count">0</span></span>
    </div>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q7">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q7">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>severity</th>
          <th>host</th><th>module</th><th>site_class</th>
          <th>locus</th><th>exec_target</th><th>matched_detections</th><th>triage_signals</th><th>snippet</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr><td colspan="9" style="text-align:center;color:var(--fg-mute)">no implant patterns detected — clean fleet</td></tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q8">
    <h2>Q8 · Recent-mtime concentration per host × module</h2>
    <p class="lede">Hosts × modules where five or more persistence records carry <code class="sig">recent_mtime_7d</code>. Spikes are either a config-management push (legitimate, correlatable with the orchestration tool's change log) or a post-compromise sweep (attacker hardening persistence after initial access). Zero rows on a quiet fleet is the expected state.</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q8">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q8">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>host</th><th>module</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">recent_records</th>
          <th data-sort-type="num">recent_loci</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr><td colspan="4" style="text-align:center;color:var(--fg-mute)">no concentrated mtime activity in the last 7 days</td></tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q9">
    <h2>Q9 · Exec targets under /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm, /run/shm</h2>
    <p class="lede">Q4 requires <em>both</em> orphan and interpreter_in_tmp; this query is the open half. Records whose <code>exec_target</code> lives in a tmp-class directory, orphan or not. The <code>is_orphan</code> column tells the analyst whether the payload is currently armed (false = armed and on disk; true = dormant or post-execution). Q9 set minus Q4 = "currently armed implants".</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q9">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q9">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>host</th><th>module</th><th>site_class</th>
          <th>locus</th><th>exec_target</th>
          <th>is_orphan</th><th>snippet</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        
          <tr>
            <td>h1</td><td>cron</td><td>cron.d</td>
            <td class="path">/etc/cron.d/singleton-impl</td>
            <td class="target">/tmp/beacon.sh</td>
            <td>yes</td>
            <td class="snippet">cron-singleton-norm</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>h2</td><td>cron</td><td>cron.d</td>
            <td class="path">/etc/cron.d/implant</td>
            <td class="target">/tmp/payload.sh</td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td class="snippet">implant-norm</td>
          </tr>
        
          <tr>
            <td>h3</td><td>cron</td><td>cron.d</td>
            <td class="path">/etc/cron.d/implant</td>
            <td class="target">/tmp/payload.sh</td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td class="snippet">implant-norm</td>
          </tr>
        
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  
  <section class="pane" data-pane="q10">
    <h2>Q10 · Per-host collection health and coverage outliers</h2>
    <p class="lede">For each host: records emitted, distinct modules, distinct site_classes. Sort descending by records to find anomaly hosts (3× their peers); ascending to find collection failures (zero or near-zero). Run this query first when investigating a new dataset — it catches silent collection breakage before any threat-hunting query can.</p>
    <input class="search" type="search" placeholder="filter rows…" data-target="#t-q10">
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="sortable" id="t-q10">
        <thead><tr>
